course: dictionary
lesson: 9405
title: Squabble: small, noisy arguments
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To squabble is to argue noisily about something small and unimportant. The clues are the noise and the small thing, like one biscuit or the seat at the front of the car.
explain: «Gulls really do squabble for every scrap» tells you that gulls make a noisy fuss over tiny bits of food. Squabble can be a verb, something people or animals do: squabble, squabbles, squabbled, squabbling. It can also be a noun: a squabble, a small silly argument.
explain: Use the swap check. In «Sea lions often squabble loudly over the best resting spots on the rocks», does "argue noisily over the best spots" fit? Yes, so that is what squabble means here.
fact: Squabble can be a verb (to squabble) or a noun (a squabble).
fact: «Squabble» means to argue noisily about something small and unimportant.
fact: «Squabbled» is the past tense: yesterday the twins squabbled over the remote.
fact: A squabble is a small, silly argument, such as who gets the front seat.
fact: Seagulls and sea lions squabble over food and the best places to rest.
exercise: «Sea lions often squabble loudly over the best resting spots on the rocks.» What does squabble mean here? | * Argue noisily about something small | Swim very fast to the sea | Climb up a tall cliff | Sleep peacefully together
exercise: In «The twins had a squabble about the TV remote», what kind of word is squabble? | * Noun | Verb | Adverb | Adjective
exercise: Which is the best example of a squabble? | A quiet walk in the park | * Two friends arguing noisily over who gets the last biscuit | A painting of a sunny field | A long journey across the sea
test: Yesterday the two cats ___ over the last piece of fish. Which word fits? | squabble | squabbling | * squabbled | squabbles
test: Which sentence uses squabble as a noun? | They squabble every day about who does the washing up | The seagulls are squabbling loudly | She will squabble with her brother later | * Their squabble about the seat lasted five minutes
exam: In «the real devil ... does screech and squabble loudly over a dead wallaby», what are the devils doing? | Sharing the wallaby kindly | * Arguing noisily over it | Sleeping beside it | Burying it quietly
